Remaining edges: {T1.2,T1.3} = b1-c1 ∈ E1 (shown). {T1.0,T1.2} = b0-b1 ∈ E1. {T2.0,T2.2} = a0-b1: ~ {T1.1,T1.2} = a0-b1 [T1-T2]; and {T1.1,T1.2} = a0-b1 ∈? Not yet assigned. a0-b1(T1) ~ a0-b1(T2). Any other identifications? Top-bottom maps {T2.0,T2.2} ↦ {T0.3,T0.1} = b0-c1 ∈ E2!!! So a0-b1(T2) ~ b0-c1(T0) ∈ E2. So E2 ⊇ {a0b1(T1), a0b1(T2), b0c1(T0), b0c1(T1), a1c1(T2), a0c0(T0)}. And {T1.0,T1.2} = b0-b1 ∈ E1 — CONTRADICTION!? b0-b1(T1) = {T1.0,T1.2} ~ {T1.1,T1.2}?? NO — {T1.0,T1.2} ~ {T1.1,T1.2} only if there's an identification mapping it. T1-T2 gluing maps {T1.0,T1.2} ↦ {T2.1,T2.2} ✓. Top-bottom maps {T2.1,T2.2} ↦ {T0.0,T0.1} ✓ ∈ E1. And T1-T2 maps {T1.1,T1.2} ↦ {T2.0,T2.2}; top-bottom maps {T2.0,T2.2} ↦ {T0.3,T0.1} ∈ E2. So b0-b1(T1) ∈ E1 and a0-b1(T1) ∈ E2 — no contradiction, different edges of T1. OK so E1 and E2 don't merge. Third: {T1.1,T1.3} = a0-c1 ∈ E1 ✓ (via T2.0-T2.3 chain? {T2.0,T2.3} ~ {T1.1,T1.3} ✓ ∈ E1). {T2.0,T2.1} ∈ E1 ✓. {T2.0,T2.3} = a0-c1 ∈ E1 ✓. {T2.1,T2.3} = a1-c1 ∈ E2 ✓. {T2.2,T2.3} = b1-c1 ∈ E1 ✓. {T0.0,T0.2} = a0-c0 ∈ E2 ✓. {T0.1,T0.2} = b0-c0 ∈ E1 ✓. {T0.0,T0.3} ∈ E1 ✓. {T0.1,T0.3} ∈ E2 ✓. {T0.2,T0.3} ∈ E1 ✓. {T1.0,T1.3} = b0-c1 ∈ E2 ✓.

